  "notes": [
    "Pass rate tied at 100% under a deliberately lenient grader that normalizes extensions before checking membership. A strict-schema grader (requiring dict-of-extension keys, exact case, etc.) would have shown a meaningful delta \u2014 baselines invented different schemas on every run.",
    "With-skill is MORE expensive here (~+1s, ~+3k tokens) because the task is simple enough that a competent model solves it without external guidance. The extra cost is the skill-read overhead (SKILL.md + classification_rules.md).",
    "Standard deviation on time is the real story: with-skill \u03c3=2.4s vs baseline \u03c3=10.0s (~4x). The skill buys *predictability* more than raw correctness.",
    "Output-schema drift in baselines: eval-0 invented a spurious '.rna.txt' extension key; eval-1 and eval-2 keyed bulk_files / sc_files by *filenames* ('cd8_effector.h5ad') rather than extensions. A downstream tool consuming these as if they were extension-keyed dicts would break.",
    "Baselines got the substantive answer right on all three evals (correct classification + correct refusal logic). The skill's strongest value in isolation is consistency; its strongest value in context is the pre-flight gate inside build-taiji-input, which this benchmark does not directly measure.",
    "Single run per configuration (N=1). The stddev reflects eval-to-eval variation rather than within-config variance. Re-running \u22653x per config would give a proper noise floor, especially since the time numbers are close."
